        "title": "Stars in their eyes",
        "text": "It is dawn in the Andes. A grimy carpet of red dust stretches over rolling, featureless hills towards a horizon dominated by the icy cone of Chile's Licancabur volcano. There are no trees, no bushes, not even a blade of grass in this lifeless landscape. Even the atmosphere is alien. At 5,000 metres above sea level, oxygen levels are tiny. Move suddenly, and bright spots blur your vision before you begin to stagger and retch. This is Chajnantor, the most inhospitable part of Chile's Atacama Desert and one of the driest places in the world. The annual rainfall here is negligible: a few drops of moisture scattered each year on dried-out, empty land. You would not last a day unprotected in this dead zone. It is hostile and threatening, and in a few years it is going to give astronomers a new home.\n\nIt sounds extraordinary. Nevertheless, the European Southern Observatory has committed itself to starting work on an array of giant microwave receivers on Chajnantor. When they are finished, these machines will allow the world's astronomers to watch the birth of thousands of planetary systems in the deepest corners of our galaxy, and to explore the early universe's \"Dark Ages\", when dust hid the cosmos and the first stars flickered into existence. Unique knowledge is expected to flow from Alma, the Atacama Large Millimetre Array.\n\nEach Alma receiver will consist of a transportable structure supporting a dish 10 metres in diameter. Extending outwards from the smooth metal surface of the dish will be three support arms that hold a sub-reflector. This will direct the radio waves down into the heart of the receiver, where signals from deep space can be amplified, recorded and analysed. To make up for the rotation of the Earth, the dish will need to be fully steerable. To change its angle, it will be mounted on an elevation structure, under which there will be a rotating structure. This will move smoothly around on the steel base, the cylindrical platform for the receiver.\n\nBuilding sixty-four of these telescopes on a vast, adjustable grid of tracks covering 40 square kilometres is a daunting task. Together, the collecting surfaces of the receivers will measure 6,000 square metres, the size of a football pitch. Putting together such an enormous piece of equipment, with its £250 million price tag, in the thin air and red dust of the central Chilean Andes seems outrageously ambitious.\n\nBut why bother? Given that the night sky is full of bright galaxies, why do astronomers build telescopes made to an accuracy of a billionth of a metre just to see faint ones? The answer is about looking back in time, says astronomer David Field of the University of Aarhus. \"The fainter the galaxy, the more distant it is. And because light travels at a finite speed, faint galaxies are also the oldest. In other words, telescopes are time machines. They have shown us what the universe was like ten billion years ago, and how it developed during its childhood.\"\n\nScientists are now satisfied that they know about the universe's birth and its childhood. But they still need to learn about its early infancy thirteen billion years ago, after the newborn cosmos had exploded into existence and the heavens had filled with superheated particles, a thin gruel of matter that somehow came together to produce heavy, solid objects. \"We want to know how such a hot, fluid universe turned into one with galaxies, stars, planets and continents and, eventually, people,\" says UK astronomer Dr Paul Murdin. \"We want to know how the heavens got their structure. In short, we want to understand how we got from the Big Bang to human beings.\" One answer is to build even bigger optical telescopes to collect photons from even more distant, and therefore older, objects. However, really distant galaxies move away from us so fast that much of their light is transformed, by an effect known as the Doppler shift, into microwave radiation.\n\nAnd that is why Chajnantor is so important. Its receivers will be built specifically to collect this precious microwave radiation and to help astronomers understand one of science's last great cosmological mysteries: the structured, solid nature of the cosmos. But collecting microwave radiation has one major drawback, as Esteban Illanes of the European Southern Observatory explains. \"It is absorbed by water. An observatory at sea level would pick up nothing. All the microwaves coming down from space would have been absorbed by clouds or water vapour in the atmosphere before they reached our instruments. That is why we picked Chajnantor. It is high and dry: perfect for picking up microwaves.\"\n\nThe choice is also especially fitting, because people have been exploiting the dryness of the Atacama Desert for thousands of years. This is where mummification, the process of drying bodies to preserve them, began. Now the special conditions that made this possible are giving scientists a chance to explain how the universe acquired the solidity that made life possible in the first place. It will be a key scientific milestone."
